# Git stores text blobs as LF and, with core.autocrlf=true, hands Windows a
# CRLF working tree. Most tools cope. The ones that do not fail in ways that
# are very hard to read: tests/backup.test.js could not be loaded at all on
# Windows — "SyntaxError: Invalid or unexpected token", no file, no line, no
# stack — because Vite's shebang strip does not account for a CR before the
# newline. Linux CI checked out LF, so it stayed green for as long as the file
# existed. Pinning eol=lf means every checkout matches what CI tests.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary formats: never inspected for line endings, never rewritten.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.ico   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.zip   binary
*.pptx  binary
*.xlsx  binary
*.xlsb  binary
